oily skin owners you know how it is your  face is immoral everything you touch  gets oily makeup slides around here are  my top skin care and makeup tips for  oily skin it's michelle of lab muffin  beauty science chemistry phd cosmetic  chemist and shiny person i live in  australia which gets hot and humid and  i'm a youtuber who has to film in front  of very hot lights so i'm pretty  experienced at dealing with oil here's  what i found works tip one the first  thing you want to check is is your skin  actually oily oily skin is genetic but  sometimes the wrong skincare routine can  irritate your skin and that can make  oily or even dry skin leak out a whole  bunch of oil so it might look like your  skin is oily even if it's not oily skin  can also be dehydrated which means it's  lacking water even if there's too much  oil this leads to a pretty common dry  but oily situation where your skin feels  tight and dry and cracked but it's also  covered in oil a lot of people who think  they have dry skin actually have  dehydrated skin unfortunately my skin is  truly oily technically oily and normal  since my cheeks are more towards normal  and having skin that's prone to becoming  dehydrated combined with a bad skincare  routine made my oily skin even oilier so  the first thing to do if you're  frustrated with too much oil is to make  sure that you're not accidentally making  the whole situation way worse if you  want a really detailed guide to doing a  skin evaluation and a rundown of the  different skin types and conditions i've  done one in my guide to basic skin care  so let's say you've confirmed that your  skin is actually oily what can you do  tip number two wash your face gently  it's very tempting to wash with harsh  cleansers to cut through the oil and try  to dry it out  don't do this this is a trap  harsh cleansers will strip away both oil  and your natural moisturizing factor or  nmf this is a collection of chemicals  that are naturally in your skin they  hold onto water and keep your skin  hydrated they're your skin's natural  humectant moisturizer harsh surfactants  can also warp the shape of proteins in  your skin leading to loss of water again  dehydrated skin now your skin has  evolved to be very responsive to its  water content when your skin's  dehydrated it's triggered to produce  more oil to keep your skin conditioned  which means more of that whole  mirrorable situation it's a vicious  cycle so gentle cleansing is the way to  break out of this if your skin feels  tight and dry after washing you are  probably overdoing it it should feel  soft and plump and if you're used to  bone dry clean skin after washing your  face  gentle washing might actually feel like  your face isn't clean it does take some  getting used to but gentle cleansing was  the number one thing that helped me and  a lot of other people with oily skin  there are a lot of things that go into  finding a gentle cleanser but the  easiest thing to do is to avoid sodium  lauryl sulfate in the top ingredients of  your cleanser and to avoid soaps i have  listed some of the common ones here  their molecules are quite slim so they  insert themselves into your skin easily  staying there and irritating it and  related to this is tip three  try washing your face less even gentle  cleansers can cause a bit of this  dehydration then oil production effect  which is why i think it's worth trying  just cleansing with cleanser once a day  i personally cleanse properly at night  and in the morning i just splash my face  with water and pat it dry with a towel  before doing the rest of my morning  routine skincare products don't just sit  on your face in one big film that blocks  everything else from mentoring i think  we tend to think of skin like glass but  it is more like a burger bun stuff sinks  in and you can't really get everything  off without also removing a lot of the  bun or your skin's components i found  that water in the morning is enough for  me and cutting down my skin's exposure  to cleanses has helped with the oil a  lot my oil ends up coming back slower  throughout the day instead of just in  one big flood plus the uncomfortable  tight feeling and greasy dehydrated but  oily situation has died down a lot  instead of having tight skin with oil on  top which was kind of like  greasy flaky plastic that had been out  in the sun for too long the oil sort of  sinks in a bit more easily and it's more  nice and dewy rather than just greasy  and a lot of people with oily skin have  found that this works for them too i do  sometimes cleanse a second time in the  middle of the day if i've been wearing a  mask or if i want to take off my makeup  early when i do that i use bioderma  micellar water which is super gentle and  designed for sensitive skin it doesn't  use proper cleansing surfactants i've  listed a few gentle cleanses that i like  in the description but remember  everyone's skin is different so it's  important to work out what your skin  likes and it's possible that your skin  does prefer to be cleansed twice a day  but i think it's worth experimenting  with this if you haven't already tip  number four sort out your moisturizing  routine it seems to make sense that if  your skin's oily it doesn't need any  more moisturizer since it produces way  too much of its own already but again  just like the cleansing thing this can  be a trap your skin's natural oil isn't  actually that good at moisturizing there  are three main ways of moisturizing your  skin there's humectants which grab onto  water and keep it in the skin occlusive  seal the water in and emollient soften  your skin your natural sebum doesn't do  the first two very well so it just  softens your skin it doesn't actually  help with water retention if your skin  is hydrated and oily then you might not  need a moisturizer you have enough of  both water and oil but if your skin is  dehydrated and oily which is the case  for a lot of people like me  then having a hydrating moisturizer that  has humectants and occlusives can keep  your skin's water content up and that  helps it control the oil i like using  lightweight products like toners with  lots of humectants during the day and  less occlusives because they can get a  bit greasy and shiny i try to use  hydrating sunscreens i also use a  moisturizer at night sometimes i layer  an occlusive product over humectant for  extra hydration  and this has all made a massive  difference tip number five salicylic  acid is great for pores salicylic acid  commonly known as beta hydroxy acid is a  chemical exfoliant and antibacterial  skin care ingredient it gets recommended  for oily skin a lot because it dissolves  in oil so in theory it's more likely to  be able to penetrate into blackheads and  clogged pores and exfoliate inside them  which means less breakouts and  blackheads now i haven't seen any actual  studies on whether salicylic acid is  better at this than other exfoliants but  it does sound pretty reasonable i've  personally found that my pores look a  lot less noticeable when i've been  consistently using salicylic acid my  favorite is paula's choice 2 bha liquid  but there are lots of different options  and if you want to find out more about  exfoliation in general i have a free  guide that i've linked down below tip 6  is to try some oil reducing skin care  unfortunately there aren't that many  skincare ingredients that have been  found to reduce oil on your face and for  the ones where we do have some evidence  there's usually only a single study so  it isn't very reliable and they might  not actually work but if you want to try  some anyway here's the stuff with the  most evidence there's one study where a  two percent niacinamide serum reduced  the amount of oil in people's faces  niacinamide is a pretty good option  because it does a lot of other stuff as  well another study found that a plant  extract of sore palmetto in a cream  reduced oiliness some vitamin a  derivatives like retinol and tretinoin  might be able to reshape the ducts that  your sebum flows out from and that can  mean it cuts down on the oil pooling on  your face clay is also good at soaking  up oil but you do need to use it  regularly to get the benefits because it  is pretty temporary although not super  temporary usually a clay mask twice a  week works pretty well tip number seven  is to get the right translucent powder  translucent powder is one of the most  essential products in my routine for  soaking up oil these powders don't look  too obvious on your face so it's really  good if you don't like to wear obvious  makeup and you can just use this over  your sunscreen as well the ingredient  that i found works best for me is starch  so i'm always looking for starch high up  in the ingredients list for any  translucent powder i've also tried using  cornstarch from the grocery store which  actually works reasonably well  although a proper product is a lot  easier to use there are also some  powders that have clay which tends to be  pretty good at soaking up oil too one  thing you can try is commonly called the  wayne goss method where you put a layer  of powder under your foundation so it  soaks up the oil before it gets to the  surface and even before it gets to your  foundation so that means it won't slide  around as much i use a big fluffy brush  to try to concentrate the powder on my  t-zone which is the forehead and the  nose and also these bits around my nose  which tend to get shiny first tip number  eight is to try dewy makeup it has been  so ingrained into my head over the years  that matters the way to go with makeup  for oily skin  just to beat the mirror ball into  submission but i've actually found that  dewy makeup works a lot better for me  when i do get oily the contrast between  the oily and the non-oily bits isn't  quite as obvious so i look more healthy  rather than patchy i usually add  highlighter on the tops of my cheeks and  the tip of my nose and a little bit of  my forehead as well then i put starch  powder lightly on top and keep topping  up that starch powder as needed  throughout the day and that seems to  work pretty well dewy makeup is also a  bit easier to blend back in with your  fingers throughout the day and this is  related to the moisturizing tip try  hydrating your skin underneath before  you put makeup on for example you can  try a hydrating toner hydrating your  skin makes it plumper and less textured  it can smooth out fine lines and reduce  flaking and that means the pigment  doesn't have as many rope bumps on your  face that can make a bunch up there are  a lot of silicone-based makeup products  and primers that are meant to absorb oil  and fill your pores but i've actually  found that these tend to make me even  greasier they're good for about half an  hour until the oil soaks into them and  then i start looking like i'm made of  melting wax it is not a good look but  silicones do work for a lot of oily skin  people on the topic of makeup tip 9 try  using less pigmented makeup this is not  going to be possible or practical for a  lot of people but it is something to try  if you haven't already using base  products like foundation that have less  coverage basically the less pigment you  have on your skin the less stuff there  is that can move around and bunch up for  example you can try using a sheer  foundation all over and then a heavier  foundation or just concealer on the bits  that need a bit more coverage i love and  i'm currently using trini london bff  serum de-stress it's a really sheer  foundation that sort of just makes your  skin look really glowy but still natural  then after that i use my fingers to  pattern the ordinary's concealer on the  bits that i don't think i covered enough  so in total there's just less pigment on  my face which means the patchiness and  bunched up bits aren't as obvious when  it happens because we know it is going  to happen you can also try using a  product like neon's photography fluid  this has light reflecting particles that  bounce light around and sort of give the  illusion of radiance and smoothness  without actually having skin colored  pigments i found that if i use this  before foundation i don't need as much  coverage and on to my final tip tip  number 10 look into medical treatments  there are a few medical treatments that  can reduce oil production too many  androgens which are male sex hormones  can cause excess oil production so  medications that reduce them like some  oral contraceptive pills and  spironolactone can also reduce oil  production isotretinoin which is better  known as accutane will also reduce oil  most of the time these aren't really  used for treating oily skin they use for  acne but reducing oil is part of how  they work if you're interested in these  obviously speak to your doctor and see  if they're suitable for you there are  also some newer treatments that are  promising for reducing oil like botox  and some laser and light treatments but  they're pretty expensive and the  evidence behind their effectiveness just  isn't super convincing yet let me know  in the comments if there's a tip i  didn't cover if these tips worked for  you like subscribe follow me on tiktok  and instagram more videos are popping up  around me if you want to note out more  on beauty science

